## Docker: packaging

The image is built from `uv.lock` (`uv sync --locked`), so the container gets exactly the
locked versions on the pinned Python. Run `uv lock` (or `uv sync`) before the first build.

```bash
docker compose build
docker compose run --rm train                                      # default run
docker compose run --rm train model=logistic_regression seed=7     # overrides as usual
docker compose run --rm train -m model=random_forest,logistic_regression
docker compose up mlflow                                           # http://localhost:5000
```

`conf/`, `data/`, `outputs/` and `mlruns/` are bind-mounted, so results land on the host and
config edits apply without rebuilding. Code or dependency changes need `docker compose build`.

Without compose:

```bash
docker build -t {{ project_slug }} .
docker run --rm -v "$PWD/data:/app/data" -v "$PWD/outputs:/app/outputs" \
  -v "$PWD/mlruns:/app/mlruns" {{ project_slug }} model=logistic_regression
```

Notes:

- MLflow stores absolute artifact paths. An experiment created inside the container points
  at `/app/mlruns/artifacts`; view it with `docker compose up mlflow`, not a host-side
  `mlflow ui`. Pick one mode per `mlruns/` folder, or point both at a shared tracking server.
- On Linux, files written to the bind mounts are owned by root. Add
  `user: "1000:1000"` (your `id -u`:`id -g`) to the `train` service if that matters.
